ROWS = [
    dict(id='T223',
 d='NNT',
 t='Global convergence of the mean-field gradient flow for two-layer networks',
 s='Let f_rho(x) = ∫ a·sigma(<w,x>) rho(da,dw) be a two-layer network written as an integral over a measure rho of neurons, and let rho_t be the Wasserstein gradient flow of the population square loss R(rho), the limit of gradient descent on a width-m network as m → ∞ under mean-field scaling. Prove that for ReLU units, Gaussian inputs, a target realizable by finitely many neurons and an initialization rho_0 such as a Gaussian (unbounded support, with a stated convention for the flow at the ReLU kink), the flow rho_t is well posed, R(rho_t) → inf R as t → ∞ without entropic regularization, and rho_t converges in W2 (these are separate questions: risk convergence does not imply convergence of the parameter law); give a rate.',
 c='Chizat–Bach prove that IF the flow converges in W2 then the limit is a global minimizer, under hypotheses that include an initialization whose support is bounded (in a ball B(0, r_b)) and separates the spheres, plus technical conditions (Theorems 3.3 and 3.5, auditor codex-w687, 16 Oct); a Gaussian initialization is outside that scope. The open part is convergence itself, for natural initializations, and any rate. With added noise (mean-field Langevin, T225) convergence is known but the rate degrades with the temperature.',
 src='L. Chizat and F. Bach, On the Global Convergence of Gradient Descent for Over-parameterized Models using Optimal Transport, arXiv:1805.09545 (2018); S. Mei, A. Montanari and P.-M. Nguyen, A Mean Field View of the Landscape of Two-Layers Neural Networks, arXiv:1804.06561 (2018)',
 loc='1805.09545 Section 3.1 Theorem 3.3 (PDF p6) and Section 3.2 Theorem 3.5 (PDF p7); 1804.06561 Section 3 Theorem 3 (PDF p12) and Section 4 (PDF p15). Read by codex-w687, 16 Oct.',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.09545',
 why="The mean-field limit is the cleanest probabilistic model of feature learning in neural networks; global convergence of its gradient flow is the basic question behind 'overparameterized networks are trained to global minima'.",
 st='Open in the stated generality. Known: global convergence under entropic regularization (Mei–Montanari–Nguyen 1804.06561; Hu et al. 1905.07769; Chizat 2202.01009), for special symmetric targets and one-dimensional settings, and the conditional theorem of Chizat–Bach. Petit, Poon and Peyré (arXiv:2605.10775) extend the conditional statement to bounded and asymptotically one-homogeneous activations with vector outputs and to multi-head attention: if the mean-field flow converges in W2, its limit is a global minimizer. Convergence of the flow itself is not proved. Open.',
 n='Fame: the Chizat–Bach and Mei–Montanari–Nguyen papers are among the most cited in deep-learning theory; the convergence gap is stated in many surveys (e.g. Bartlett–Montanari–Rakhlin 2103.09177).',
 a=True),
    dict(id='T224',
 d='NNT',
 t='Uniform-in-time weak mean-field rates when noiseless training converges slowly',
 s='In the two-layer mean-field gradient-flow model of arXiv:2605.22010, initialize m neurons iid from the same law as the limiting flow and use the paper’s regularity assumptions. Suppose its excess population MSE L_t<=C(1+t)^{-c}, with 0<c<=2, so that integral_0^infinity sqrt(L_t)dt need not be finite. Does this decay assumption alone imply sup_{t>=0} E||f_{rho_t^m}-f_{rho_t}||_{L^2(P_X)}^2<=poly(d)m^{-a(c)} for some a(c)>0? Determine the sharp exponent, or exhibit the missing stability hypothesis/counterexample.',
 c='This is an output L^2 weak error, not Wasserstein distance between a discrete empirical measure and a continuous parameter law. The source theorem is not restated as an open problem.',
 src='Uniform-in-Time Weak Propagation-of-Chaos in Shallow Neural Networks (arXiv:2605.22010); Mei-Montanari-Nguyen (arXiv:1804.06561).',
 loc='2605.22010, abstract, Section 2 dynamics and the convergence/integrability hypothesis.',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.22010',
 why='Without uniform-in-time control the mean-field theory says nothing about networks trained to convergence; this is the bridge from the infinite-width picture to the finite networks actually trained.',
 st='2605.22010 already proves noiseless uniform-in-time weak propagation of chaos using quantitative convergence and an integrable square-root-loss condition. It does not give the proposed statement merely from a slow, possibly nonintegrable loss decay.',
 n='Fame: a standard open question of the mean-field theory, raised in the founding papers. Restated 12 Nov 2026 from the T400 run (codex_local_1108/briefs/T400REP/restated.jsonl), checked by the integrator (codex_local_1108/briefs/T400REP/AUDIT_claude-agent_2026_11_12.md). Reason: The listed W2 statement is false at time zero (quantization); the open problem is the slow mean-field rate.',
 a='Statement rewritten on 12 Nov 2026 from the T400 run (restated.jsonl); awaiting re-audit.'),
    dict(id='T225',
 d='NNT',
 t='Convergence time of mean-field Langevin dynamics at low temperature',
 s='Mean-field Langevin dynamics minimizes F_lambda(rho) = R(rho) + lambda·KL(rho || pi) for a two-layer network risk R, where pi is a confining reference measure (e.g. a Gaussian prior, equivalently an L2 weight penalty plus entropy; with Lebesgue entropy on unbounded parameters F_lambda is unbounded below: spreading the output weights a as N(0, s²) independently of w leaves f_rho = 0 and the risk fixed while the entropy goes to −∞). It converges exponentially at a rate set by a uniform log-Sobolev constant of the proximal Gibbs measures, which in general is exp(−C/lambda). For a realizable two-layer regression problem in dimension d (smooth activation, Gaussian inputs, a teacher with a bounded number of neurons and bounded weights, all of which the bound may depend on polynomially), decide whether the time for MFLD (with lambda tuned or annealed) to reach risk R ≤ inf R + epsilon is polynomial in d and 1/epsilon, or prove an exponential lower bound.',
 c="The question is about the unregularized risk reached through small lambda, not the regularized objective at fixed lambda. Chizat's theorems exclude the vanilla unbounded two-layer parameterization and note a smoothness issue for ReLU, so they do not cover plain ReLU (auditor codex-w687, 16 Oct).",
 src='L. Chizat, Mean-Field Langevin Dynamics: Exponential Convergence and Annealing, arXiv:2202.01009 (2022); A. Nitanda, D. Wu and T. Suzuki, Convex Analysis of the Mean Field Langevin Dynamics, arXiv:2201.10469 (2022)',
 loc='2202.01009 Theorem 3.2 (Section 3, PDF p6), Theorem 4.1 (Section 4, PDF p7), discussion of Eq. (22) (Section 5.2, PDF p11); 2201.10469 Theorem 1. Read by codex-w687, 16 Oct.',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.01009',
 why='MFLD is the one setting where global convergence of neural-network training is fully proved; whether its guarantees are ever efficient decides whether that proof says anything quantitative.',
 st='Open. Exponential-in-1/lambda LSI constants are what the general theory gives; annealing gives only logarithmic convergence. Some structured targets (e.g. single-index) have polynomial guarantees in later work (to verify).',
 n='Fame: central question of the MFLD line (Chizat; Nitanda–Wu–Suzuki; Suzuki et al.).',
 a=True),
    dict(id='T226',
 d='NNT',
 t='The edge of stability: why full-batch gradient descent hovers at sharpness 2/eta',
 s='For full-batch gradient descent with fixed step eta on a neural-network training loss, the top Hessian eigenvalue first rises to about 2/eta and then hovers there while the loss keeps decreasing non-monotonically (Cohen et al.). Fix a model (e.g. a smooth two-layer network of moderate width on Gaussian data with square loss, or a deep linear network), an initialization law and a range of eta. Prove that with probability bounded below over the initialization GD enters the edge-of-stability regime, stays there, and converges to a global minimizer whose sharpness is 2/eta + o(1) as eta → 0 (or show the event has small probability). Local theorems near a minimizing manifold (Gan 2026, Theorem 4.4, assumes a C4 loss and a nondegeneracy condition) leave open whether random initialization reaches their basin.',
 c='Results that assume a specific cubic structure of the loss, or study two-parameter toy models, are partial. Divergence above the classical 2/L threshold is NOT what happens: the point is convergence without the descent-lemma condition.',
 src='J. M. Cohen, S. Kaur, Y. Li, J. Z. Kolter and A. Talwalkar, Gradient Descent on Neural Networks Typically Occurs at the Edge of Stability, arXiv:2103.00065 (2021)',
 loc='2103.00065 Sections 1-3 (empirical); E. Gan, arXiv:2606.15551 (2026), Theorem 4.4 (local convergence at the threshold). Read by codex-w687, 16 Oct.',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.00065',
 why='Classical optimization theory says GD with step above 2/L diverges; every practical network is trained in exactly that regime. A theory of it is one of the main gaps between optimization theory and practice.',
 st='Partial: self-stabilization under assumptions on third derivatives (Damian, Nichani and Lee 2209.15594); Arora, Li and Panigrahi 2205.09745 for normalized losses; Ahn et al. 2204.01050; analyses of two-layer and scalar toy models. Open for genuine networks (not re-checked against 2025–26).',
 n='Fame: one of the most discussed empirical phenomena of deep-learning optimization since 2021.',
 a=True),
    dict(id='T227',
 d='NNT',
 t='The catapult phase of large-learning-rate training',
 s='Lewkowycz et al. observe for wide networks three regimes in the step eta relative to lambda_0, the top eigenvalue of the neural tangent kernel at initialization: lazy for eta < 2/lambda_0, a catapult phase for 2/lambda_0 < eta < eta_max ≈ c/lambda_0 in which the loss first grows, the curvature drops, and training then converges, and divergence above eta_max. Prove the existence of the catapult phase and identify eta_max for a fixed deep nonlinear architecture of large finite width (stated activation, input and label law, normalization of the initialization), as an eta interval on which the loss rises and then converges with high probability.',
 c="The two-layer linear 'uv' model is solved in the original paper; the question is about nonlinear and deep networks.",
 src='A. Lewkowycz, Y. Bahri, E. Dyer, J. Sohl-Dickstein and G. Gur-Ari, The large learning rate phase of deep learning: the catapult mechanism, arXiv:2003.02218 (2020)',
 loc="2003.02218 Sections 2-3 (the solvable uv model, eta_max = 4/lambda_0 there) and the Figure 3 discussion, PDF p7: for the ReLU networks of the experiments, 'eta_max ≈ 12/lambda_0 is a good predictor' (an empirical value with that paper's normalization). Corrected by codex-w687, 16 Oct.",
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.02218',
 why='Explains why large learning rates give flatter minima and better generalization; it is the infinite-width counterpart of the edge of stability (T226).',
 st='Proved for the uv model and some quadratic models (Zhu et al. 2022-23, to verify). Open for deep nonlinear networks.',
 n='Fame: widely cited (catapult mechanism), a standard example in large-learning-rate theory.',
 a=True),
    dict(id='T228',
 d='NNT',
 t='Heavy-tailed stationary behaviour of SGD on neural-network losses',
 s='Simsekli et al. observed that SGD gradient noise in deep networks is heavy-tailed, and Gurbuzbalaban, Simsekli and Zhu proved for linear regression that the SGD iterates converge to a stationary law with power-law tails whose index alpha decreases as eta/b (step over batch) grows. The same paper states a criterion for nonlinear recursions (its Theorem 1, Mirek-type asymptotically linear random maps). Verify it for a concrete nonlinear model: e.g. constant-step SGD with batch b on a generalized linear model or a two-layer network with a smooth bounded activation, Gaussian inputs and persistent label noise. Prove that the iterates have a stationary law, that the recursion contracts on average and is asymptotically linear with a non-arithmetic multiplier and a nonzero tail constant, so that the stationary tail is a power law with index given by the Lyapunov equation, with alpha < 2 in a nonempty range of (eta, b).',
 c="For linear regression the result follows from Kesten's theory of random affine recursions; nonlinear networks give non-affine random iterations. Monotonicity of the index in eta/b is proved under Gaussian data (assumption A3) and alpha >= 1 (auditor codex-w687, 16 Oct).",
 src='M. Gurbuzbalaban, U. Simsekli and L. Zhu, The Heavy-Tail Phenomenon in SGD, arXiv:2006.04740 (2020); U. Simsekli, L. Sagun and M. Gurbuzbalaban, A Tail-Index Analysis of Stochastic Gradient Noise in Deep Neural Networks, arXiv:1901.06053 (2019)',
 loc='2006.04740 Theorem 1 (PDF p4); Theorems 2-4 and Proposition 5 (PDF pp7-8). Read by codex-w687, 16 Oct.',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.04740',
 why="Heavy tails change what 'SGD finds' (Lévy-driven rather than Brownian exploration) and have been tied to generalization; a theorem for nonlinear models would turn an empirical claim into probability.",
 st='Linear case proved; nonlinear results under strong assumptions only (to verify). Open in general.',
 n="Fame: connects SGD to Kesten's random recursion theory; widely cited line.",
 a=True),
    dict(id='T229',
 d='NNT',
 t='Where label-noise SGD goes on the manifold of interpolating minima',
 s='For an overparameterized loss whose zero set is a manifold Gamma, SGD with label noise and small step eta, run for time of order eta^{-2}, follows in the limit eta → 0 a deterministic flow on Gamma that decreases tr(∇²L) (Blanc et al.; Damian et al.; Li, Wang and Arora). Gatmiry, Li, Reddi and Jegelka (arXiv:2410.16401, Theorem 3.5) prove that the flow reaches a global minimizer of the sharpness for an explicit class of smooth two-layer networks under their Assumptions 3.1, 3.2 and 3.4. Prove the same for a class outside those assumptions (two-layer ReLU networks, or networks of depth L >= 3) on generic data, or give a non-asymptotic bound on the distance to the sharpness minimizer at finite eta.',
 c='The limit theorem is known under smoothness and manifold assumptions; the open parts are the global behaviour of the limiting flow and finite-eta control.',
 src='Z. Li, T. Wang and S. Arora, What Happens after SGD Reaches Zero Loss? A Mathematical Framework, arXiv:2110.06914 (2021); A. Damian, T. Ma and J. D. Lee, Label Noise SGD Provably Prefers Flat Global Minimizers, arXiv:2106.06530 (2021); G. Blanc et al., arXiv:1904.09080 (2019)',
 loc='to verify: Li–Wang–Arora, the limiting-flow theorem and the open directions in the conclusion.',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.06914',
 why='This is the cleanest mechanism proposed for the implicit regularization of SGD noise toward flat minima.',
 st='Limiting flow proved (Li–Wang–Arora, Corollary 5.2); convergence to stationary points of a regularized loss (Damian–Ma–Lee); global sharpness minimization for an explicit smooth two-layer class (Gatmiry et al. 2024). A two-phase analysis for two-layer over-parameterized linear networks (arXiv:2603.10397) adds the linear case. Open for ReLU and deeper networks and at finite eta (auditor codex-w687, 16 Oct).',
 n='Fame: well known line on SGD implicit regularization (Blanc et al.; HaoChen et al.; Damian et al.; Li et al.).',
 a=True),
    dict(id='T230',
 d='NNT',
 t='Sample complexity of gradient descent with sample reuse for single-index models',
 s="Let y = g(<w*,x>) + noise with x ~ N(0, I_d) and unknown unit w*. Online SGD on a two-layer network needs n ≍ d^{max(1, k*−1)} samples, k* the information exponent of g (Ben Arous, Gheissari and Jagannath). Statistical-query lower bounds give n ≳ d^{max(1, k_gen/2)}, k_gen the generative exponent (Damian et al. 2024). Fix the class: g a polynomial link with label noise of a fixed law (the generative exponent depends on the conditional law of y), recovery of w* up to the link's symmetries, and a standard two-layer network with no preprocessing of the labels, trained by multi-pass SGD with a stated batch size, step-size schedule and number of passes over n samples. Determine whether it learns w* with n = d^{max(1, k_gen/2)+o(1)} samples, or prove it cannot.",
 c='Algorithms that transform the labels before running a spectral or tensor method are known to reach the SQ bound; the question is about ordinary gradient training.',
 src='G. Ben Arous, R. Gheissari and A. Jagannath, Online stochastic gradient descent on non-convex losses from high-dimensional inference, arXiv:2003.10409 (2020); A. Damian, L. Pillaud-Vivien, J. D. Lee and J. Bruna, Computational-Statistical Gaps in Gaussian Single-Index Models, arXiv:2403.05529 (2024)',
 loc="to verify: Damian et al. 2403.05529, the introduction's open questions on whether gradient methods reach the generative-exponent bound.",
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.05529',
 why="Single-index models are the standard probe of what gradient training can learn in high dimension; this decides whether 'data reuse' changes the sample-complexity exponent of SGD.",
 st='Partial: reuse and large steps already reach part of the range for specific algorithms (Lee, Oko, Suzuki and Wu, arXiv:2406.01581, Theorems 1-2; arXiv:2510.21020, Theorem 3.3, learning-rate phase transitions from the information to the generative exponent; full-batch GD versus one-pass SGD, arXiv:2602.02431, Theorem 3.1; Dandi et al. 2024). Smoothed online SGD matches the CSQ bound d^{k*/2} (Damian et al. 2305.10633). Full question open as far as the writer knows.',
 n='Fame: a central current question of high-dimensional learning theory (Ben Arous; Bruna; Lee; Krzakala–Zdeborová groups).',
 a=True),
    dict(id='T231',
 d='NNT',
 t='The leap-complexity conjecture for SGD on multi-index functions',
 s='For a function f(x) = h(x_1,...,x_P) of P coordinates of x uniform on {−1,1}^d (or Gaussian), Abbe, Boix-Adserà and Misiakiewicz define the leap complexity Leap(h). Their Conjecture 1: for fixed P = O_d(1), h outside a measure-zero set of coefficients, and a rotationally invariant initialization, online SGD on a two-layer network learns f with Θ̃(d^{max(Leap(h)−1, 1)}) samples (sample or iteration complexity; arithmetic time is separate). Prove the conjecture for standard joint training, or for a first finite family of supports not covered by their layer-wise theorem.',
 c='The authors prove the upper bound for a modified layer-wise training procedure and a CSQ lower bound; the conjecture is about standard joint SGD training.',
 src='E. Abbe, E. Boix-Adserà and T. Misiakiewicz, SGD learning on neural networks: leap complexity and saddle-to-saddle dynamics, arXiv:2302.11055 (2023); The merged-staircase property, arXiv:2202.08658 (2022)',
 loc='2302.11055 Conjecture 1 (PDF p6); Theorem 1 (PDF pp11-12) and Theorem 2. Corrected by codex-w687, 16 Oct: the conjecture carries genericity, fixed-P and initialization hypotheses, and does not assert one plateau per leap for every support.',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.11055',
 why='Gives a single combinatorial quantity predicting what SGD can learn in polynomial time; proving it would be a structure theorem for feature learning.',
 st='Open for standard training; proved for leap one (merged staircase, 2202.08658) under layer-wise training and in several special cases (to verify).',
 n='Fame: a named conjecture from a widely cited 2023 COLT paper.',
 a=True),
    dict(id='T232',
 d='NNT',
 t='Saddle-to-saddle dynamics of deep linear networks from small initialization',
 s='Train a deep linear network W_L···W_1 by gradient flow on a square loss from initialization scale alpha → 0. Jacot et al. prove the escape from the saddle at the origin; the escape time is of order log(1/alpha) for L = 2 and of order alpha^{-(L-2)} for L > 2 (their Theorem 2). Their Conjecture 3: under genericity assumptions on the singular values of the data, the trajectory passes in the limit near a sequence of saddles that are local minima of the loss restricted to width k = 1, 2, ..., along translated paths. Prove the next step (the passage from the width-1 to the width-2 saddle) for general data and depth L ≥ 2 without commuting or diagonal assumptions, or the whole sequence.',
 c='Pesme and Flammarion prove the analogous picture for diagonal linear networks.',
 src='A. Jacot, F. Ged, B. Simsek, C. Hongler and F. Gabriel, Saddle-to-Saddle Dynamics in Deep Linear Networks: Small Initialization Training, Symmetry, and Sparsity, arXiv:2106.15933 (2021); S. Pesme and N. Flammarion, Saddle-to-Saddle Dynamics in Diagonal Linear Networks, arXiv:2304.00488 (2023)',
 loc='2106.15933 Theorem 2 and the paragraph after it (PDF p5), Conjecture 3 (PDF p6), Appendix C.5. Corrected by codex-w687, 16 Oct (the escape time is logarithmic only for L = 2).',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.15933',
 why='Incremental learning (one feature at a time) is the mechanism behind low-rank implicit bias; this is the basic solvable case.',
 st='Diagonal case proved; first steps for the general case proved by Jacot et al.; Rawal and DeWeese (arXiv:2605.01288) control the finite-width gradient flow of deep nonlinear networks through the first escape and give its time scaling. The full sequence is open.',
 n='Fame: a stated conjecture in a well-cited paper, part of the implicit-bias program.',
 a=True),
    dict(id='T233',
 d='NNT',
 t='The linear width-depth band for Gaussian deep-linear initialization',
 s='For f(x)=alpha W_L...W_1 x with Gaussian iid N(0,1) weights, hidden width m and alpha=(m^{L-1}d_y)^{-1/2}, train square loss by gradient descent with a Du-Hu admissible step of order 1/L. Fix a nondegenerate finite data set, its rank/condition number, input/output dimensions and confidence 1-delta. For every fixed c>0, does m=ceil(cL) give convergence to loss epsilon in time polynomial in L and log(1/epsilon), with probability at least 1-delta? Characterize any nontrivial constant-c obstruction.',
 c='The stronger proposed identity log T=Theta(L/m)+O(log L) is removed. This asks about Gaussian initialization, not an orthogonal or residual initialization.',
 src='Du-Hu, Width Provably Matters in Optimization for Deep Linear Neural Networks (arXiv:1901.08572); Shamir (arXiv:1809.08587).',
 loc='1901.08572, Gaussian initialization, normalization and sufficient width; contrast with depth-independent width and 2502.02531 asymptotic dynamics.',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.08572',
 why='Deep linear networks are the simplest model where depth makes optimization hard; the width-depth trade-off for random initialization is a basic question about products of random matrices in training.',
 st='Depth-independent Gaussian width is not an open claim. Du-Hu give sufficient linear-in-depth width with data-dependent factors. Asymptotic random-data dynamics do not supply a finite-width high-probability theorem for every fixed c in this band.',
 n='Fame: a standard open question of deep linear network theory. Restated 12 Nov 2026 from the T400 run (codex_local_1108/briefs/T400REP/restated.jsonl), checked by the integrator (codex_local_1108/briefs/T400REP/AUDIT_claude-agent_2026_11_12.md). Reason: Depth-independent width for Gaussian initialization was answered in the negative in 2020; the open problem is the band m ~ cL.',
 a='Statement rewritten on 12 Nov 2026 from the T400 run (restated.jsonl); awaiting re-audit.'),
    dict(id='T234',
 d='NNT',
 t='The implicit bias of gradient descent in matrix sensing with small initialization',
 s='Matrix sensing: y_i = <A_i, M*> with symmetric Gaussian A_i, i <= n, and M* PSD of rank r in R^(d x d); fit M = U U^T with U in R^(d x d) by gradient flow from U_0 = alpha U_init. Jin, Li, Lyu, Du and Lee (arXiv:2301.11500, Theorem 4.1) prove that as alpha -> 0, with time of order log(1/alpha), the trajectory follows greedy low-rank learning and recovers M* when the measurements satisfy a restricted isometry property of the order they need; Gunasekar et al. (arXiv:1705.09280) conjecture convergence to the minimum nuclear norm solution. For n Gaussian measurements below what that RIP hypothesis requires but above the information-theoretic order d r, decide whether the alpha -> 0 limit of gradient flow is the minimum-rank solution, the minimum-nuclear-norm solution, or neither, and whether it equals M*; and state the same for gradient descent with a fixed small step, which can differ from the flow.',
 c='Gunasekar et al. conjectured convergence to the minimum nuclear-norm solution; Razin and Cohen gave settings where gradient flow drives the nuclear norm to infinity and the bias is better read as rank minimization. The open problem is the full characterization.',
 src='S. Gunasekar, B. Woodworth, S. Bhojanapalli, B. Neyshabur and N. Srebro, Implicit Regularization in Matrix Factorization, arXiv:1705.09280 (2017); N. Razin and N. Cohen, Implicit Regularization in Deep Learning May Not Be Explainable by Norms, arXiv:2005.06398 (2020); S. Arora, N. Cohen, W. Hu and Y. Luo, arXiv:1905.13655 (2019)',
 loc='to verify: Gunasekar et al., the stated conjecture in Section 1; Razin–Cohen, the counterexample setting.',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.09280',
 why="Matrix sensing is the model problem for implicit regularization; its answer decides whether 'gradient descent minimizes a norm' or 'gradient descent minimizes rank' is the right picture.",
 st='Nuclear-norm form refuted in some settings (Razin–Cohen); greedy rank picture proved under assumptions (Li, Luo and Lyu 2021; later work — to verify). Full characterization open.',
 n='Fame: the Gunasekar conjecture is one of the best-known conjectures of implicit-bias theory.',
 a=True),
    dict(id='T235',
 d='NNT',
 t='A proof of grokking for modular arithmetic under standard training',
 s='Modular addition: inputs are the one-hot codes of (a, b) in Z_p x Z_p, a two-layer network of width m with a stated activation (quadratic or ReLU) and p output logits, Gaussian initialization of a stated scale, cross-entropy loss, a training set that is a uniformly random fraction rho of the p^2 pairs, full-batch gradient descent with step eta and weight decay lambda. Prove that for rho in a stated range, as p -> infinity, the training error reaches 0 by a time T_1 = O(log p / eta) while the test accuracy stays at chance + o(1) until a time c/(eta lambda), and exceeds 1 - o(1) after C log p/(eta lambda), and that the hidden weights then concentrate on Fourier features of Z_p. Lyu et al. (arXiv:2311.18817, Theorems 3.4-3.5) prove delayed generalization from a dichotomy of early (kernel) and late (small-norm) implicit biases for homogeneous networks with large initialization and small weight decay; the transformer and AdamW experiment of Power et al. (arXiv:2201.02177) is a different model.',
 c='Theorems exist for modified settings (large initialization, homogeneous networks, specific losses); the question is the standard grokking setting of Power et al.',
 src='A. Power, Y. Burda, H. Edwards, I. Babuschkin and V. Misra, Grokking: Generalization Beyond Overfitting on Small Algorithmic Datasets, arXiv:2201.02177 (2022); K. Lyu, J. Jin, Z. Li, S. S. Du, J. D. Lee and W. Hu, Dichotomy of Early and Late Phase Implicit Biases Can Provably Induce Grokking, arXiv:2311.18817 (2023)',
 loc='to verify: Lyu et al., introduction (what is proved and what remains for standard settings).',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.02177',
 why='Grokking is the most famous example of delayed generalization; a proof in its native setting would be a test case for any theory of training dynamics.',
 st='Partial (Lyu et al. 2311.18817; Kumar et al. 2310.06110; max-margin features for modular addition, Morwani et al. 2311.07568). Kumar and Sankaranarayanan (arXiv:2610.22593) prove, for one-hidden-layer ReLU networks on modular addition from constructed initializations and with a modified optimizer, finite-window theorems in which held-out accuracy rises while training cross-entropy stays nearly flat. The native setting (standard initialization and optimizer) is open.',
 n='Fame: grokking is a widely known phenomenon (2022 onward).',
 a=True),
    dict(id='T236',
 d='NNT',
 t='Mild overparameterization suffices to learn a planted ReLU teacher',
 s="Inputs x ~ N(0, I_d); teacher y = sum_{j<=k} relu(v_j . x) with orthonormal v_j and output weights 1, the model in which Safran and Shamir (arXiv:1712.08968) find spurious local minima for an exactly matched student (k >= 6) and observe that mild over-parameterization helps. Student sum_{i<=k'} relu(w_i . x) with output weights fixed to 1, square loss, Gaussian initialization of a stated scale, online SGD (or gradient descent on the population loss) with a constant step. Prove that for k' = k + c with a fixed c, or k' = k polylog(k), the student reaches population loss epsilon with probability at least 1 - delta in time and samples poly(d, k, 1/epsilon, log(1/delta)); or show that a student of width k + c still fails with probability bounded below. The absence of bad local minima for the wider student is a separate, weaker question.",
 c="With exactly k student units spurious local minima are common and GD often fails (Safran and Shamir). With k' polynomially larger than k, kernel-regime results apply but give poor sample complexity.",
 src='I. Safran and O. Shamir, Spurious Local Minima are Common in Two-Layer ReLU Neural Networks, arXiv:1712.08968 (2017)',
 loc='to verify: Safran–Shamir, final section on the effect of overparameterization (stated as open).',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.08968',
 why='Teacher-student learning is the simplest test of whether gradient descent can learn a network; the role of mild overparameterization is a long-standing question.',
 st="Open as far as the writer knows; results for k' = k + 1 in restricted settings (Safran, Yehudai and Shamir 2021, to verify).",
 n='Fame: a well-known open question since Safran–Shamir 2017.',
 a=True),
    dict(id='T237',
 d='NNT',
 t='The minimal overparameterization for gradient descent to fit n samples',
 s='Two-layer network f(x) = m^(-1/2) sum_{j<=m} a_j sigma(w_j . x / sqrt d) with a fixed smooth activation (or ReLU), output weights a_j = +-1 fixed, first layer trained, no biases, w_j iid N(0, I_d); n samples x_i iid uniform on the sphere of radius sqrt d with arbitrary labels bounded by 1; square loss; gradient descent with a stated step; n of order d^k for a fixed k >= 1. Prove that with probability at least 1 - delta gradient descent reaches training loss epsilon in poly(n, log(1/epsilon)) steps as soon as m d >= C n log^a(n) log(1/delta) for an absolute a (the counting bound up to logs), or show that more width is needed. The neural tangent kernel is well conditioned at m d of order n up to logs (Bombari, Amani and Mondelli, arXiv:2205.10217, Theorem 4; see T253), but the existing gradient-descent guarantees (Oymak and Soltanolkotabi, arXiv:1902.04674, Theorem 2.1) need more width.',
 c='Neural-tangent-kernel proofs need m ≥ poly(n) (quadratic and later subquadratic in n); the conjectured optimum is linear in the number of samples.',
 src='S. Oymak and M. Soltanolkotabi, Towards moderate overparameterization: global convergence guarantees for training shallow neural networks, arXiv:1902.04674 (2019); C. Song, A. Ramezani-Kebrya, T. Pethick, A. Eftekhari and V. Cevher, Subquadratic Overparameterization for Shallow Neural Networks, arXiv:2111.01875 (2021); S. S. Du et al., arXiv:1810.02054 (2018)',
 loc='to verify: Oymak–Soltanolkotabi, discussion of the gap to md ≈ n.',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.04674',
 why="Global convergence of GD is proved only for absurd widths; the natural conjecture that 'parameters ≈ samples' suffices would bring theory to the regime of practice.",
 st='Open; best known bounds subquadratic in n (to verify current record).',
 n='Fame: stated as the key open problem in many overparameterization papers.',
 a=True),
    dict(id='T238',
 d='NNT',
 t='The transition between the lazy and the rich regime',
 s='Single-index teacher y = g(v . x), x ~ N(0, I_d), |v| = 1; student f(x) = alpha m^(-1) sum_{j<=m} a_j relu(w_j . x) minus its value at initialization, with Gaussian initialization, square loss on n samples, gradient flow up to time T; limits m -> infinity first, then n, d -> infinity with n/d^k fixed. Chizat, Oyallon and Bach (arXiv:1812.07956) show lazy (linearized) training as alpha -> infinity; Woodworth et al. (arXiv:2002.09277) describe the transition for diagonal linear networks. Determine the window of alpha, as a function of d and n, across which the distance between the trained predictor and the linearized (kernel) predictor goes from o(1) to order 1, and the test error across that window.',
 c='Woodworth et al. solve the analogous question for diagonal linear networks; Chizat, Oyallon and Bach establish the lazy regime for large alpha.',
 src='B. Woodworth et al., Kernel and Rich Regimes in Overparametrized Models, arXiv:2002.09277 (2020); L. Chizat, E. Oyallon and F. Bach, On Lazy Training in Differentiable Programming, arXiv:1812.07956 (2018)',
 loc='to verify: Woodworth et al., discussion section (extension beyond diagonal linear networks posed as open).',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.09277',
 why='Whether a trained network behaves like a kernel or learns features is the main dichotomy of deep-learning theory; the crossover is where real networks seem to live.',
 st='Diagonal linear case solved; partial results for two-layer networks (to verify). Open.',
 n='Fame: the lazy/rich dichotomy is standard in the field.',
 a=True),
    dict(id='T239',
 d='NNT',
 t='Dynamical mean-field theory for gradient descent on two-layer networks in the proportional regime',
 s='Teacher y = sum_{j<=k} sigma(v_j . x / sqrt d) plus noise, x ~ N(0, I_d), n samples; student of width p with trained first layer and fixed readout scaled by 1/p; square loss with a stated ridge; full-batch gradient flow; n/d -> alpha. Rigorous dynamical mean-field (DMFT) limits of the training and test errors are known when the widths p and k stay fixed as d grows (Celentano, Cheng and Montanari; Gerbelot et al. arXiv:2302.05882, Theorems 3.1-3.5; arXiv:2602.06320, Theorem 3.2). Prove the finite-horizon DMFT limit, on every bounded time interval, in the extensive-width regime p/d -> beta and k/d -> beta_* with fixed beta, beta_* > 0. The long-time limit and its commutation with d -> infinity are a separate question (see T266).',
 c='DMFT is proved for generalized linear models and fixed-width networks with many samples (Celentano, Cheng and Montanari 2021; Gerbelot et al.). The proportional-width case is open.',
 src='L. Arnaboldi, L. Stephan, F. Krzakala and B. Loureiro, From high-dimensional & mean-field dynamics to dimensionless ODEs, arXiv:2302.05882 (2023); S. Goldt, M. Advani, A. Saxe, F. Krzakala and L. Zdeborová, arXiv:1906.08632 (2019)',
 loc='to verify: open directions in Arnaboldi et al. (extensive width).',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.05882',
 why='An exact, rigorous description of training curves in the regime where width, data and dimension are comparable would be the analogue of the random-features double-descent theory for trained networks.',
 st='Open as far as the writer knows; physics-level predictions exist.',
 n='Fame: part of the statistical-physics program for learning dynamics.',
 a=True),
    dict(id='T240',
 d='NNT',
 t='Escape from the symmetric plateau in teacher-student committee machines',
 s='Soft committee machine with K = M = 2: student f(x) = g(w_1 . x/sqrt N) + g(w_2 . x/sqrt N), teacher y = g(v_1 . x/sqrt N) + g(v_2 . x/sqrt N), g(z) = erf(z/sqrt 2), output weights fixed to 1, v_n . v_m/N = delta_nm, x ~ N(0, I_N), noiseless labels; online SGD with one fresh sample per step and step eta/sqrt N, time t = steps/N; initialization iid uniform on the sphere of radius sqrt(N q0). For each eta in an interval where the symmetric fixed point of the Saad-Solla ODE is hyperbolic with one unstable direction (eigenvalue lambda(eta)), prove that the specialization time tau_N = inf{t : |R_11 - R_12| > delta} satisfies tau_N / log N -> 1/(2 lambda(eta)) in probability and that the generalization error tends to 0 after tau_N; then K > M >= 2 and other activations.',
 c='The ODE limit itself is proved (Goldt et al. 2019); the global behaviour of the ODE system is the open part.',
 src='S. Goldt, M. Advani, A. M. Saxe, F. Krzakala and L. Zdeborová, Dynamics of stochastic gradient descent for two-layer neural networks in the teacher-student setup, arXiv:1906.08632 (2019)',
 loc='to verify: Section 2 (ODE theorem) and the discussion of plateaus.',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.08632',
 why='Plateaus are the classic statistical-physics picture of learning (Saad–Solla 1995); proving them closes a thirty-year-old loop between physics and rigorous probability.',
 st='Open in general; special cases (K = M = 1, 2) analysed (to verify).',
 n='Fame: Saad–Solla dynamics is a classic of the physics of learning.',
 a=True),
    dict(id='T241',
 d='NNT',
 t='Global convergence of feature-learning infinite-width limits of deep networks',
 s="Three-layer network of width m in the maximal-update parametrization of Yang and Hu (arXiv:2011.14522): f(x) = m^(-1) a . phi(W_2 phi(W_1 x)), W_1 entries N(0, 1/d), W_2 entries N(0, 1/m), a entries N(0, 1), learning rates scaled as in muP, a smooth activation phi; n fixed training points in a fixed dimension d with a regression target; square loss; gradient flow. As m -> infinity the dynamics converge to Yang and Hu's limiting equations, which learn features. Prove that for every target in a stated class and data in general position, the limiting gradient flow drives the training loss to 0 as t -> infinity (or converges to a global minimizer of a stated population risk), or exhibit an initialization-typical stationary point where it stalls. Compare with the three-layer mean-field limit of T367 first.",
 c='For L = 2 this is the mean-field problem T223. For deeper networks even well-posedness of the mean-field dynamics needs care (Nguyen–Pham; Sirignano–Spiliopoulos).',
 src='G. Yang and E. J. Hu, Feature Learning in Infinite-Width Neural Networks (Tensor Programs IV), arXiv:2011.14522 (2020)',
 loc='to verify: open problems in the conclusion of Tensor Programs IV.',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.14522',
 why='muP is used to tune very large models; a convergence theorem for its limit would say that the training rule practitioners use has a well-behaved infinite-width limit.',
 st='Open as far as the writer knows (results with special initializations for multilayer mean-field, Nguyen–Pham 2020, to verify).',
 n='Fame: Tensor Programs IV is one of the most cited theory papers of recent years.',
 a=True),
    dict(id='T242',
 d='NNT',
 t='Commuting the depth and width limits of residual-network training',
 s='For ResNets with two-layer-perceptron blocks h^l = h^(l-1) + (ML)^(-1) V_l phi(D^(-1/2) U_l^T h^(l-1)) (depth L, hidden width M, embedding dimension D) in the maximal-local-update regime, Chaintron, Chizat and Maass prove that k steps of (clipped) gradient descent converge to a single large-scale limit at rate O(1/L + sqrt(D/(ML)) + 1/sqrt D) whenever log L <= D <= C M, so the depth and width limits commute when M is of order D. Prove convergence to the same limit when D/M → ∞ with D/(ML) → 0 (their constraint D = O(M) removed), or a bound with at most exponential dependence on the number of steps k (theirs is super-exponential); or prove the joint limit of training for depth-one residual blocks with branch multiplier 1/sqrt(L) and muP width scaling (Bordelon et al.; Yang et al.), where only the sequential limit and the first forward pass are rigorous.',
 c="Repaired after audit (codex-w687, 16 Oct: the first version, 'jointly in any manner' for an unspecified model, is answered for depth-two blocks with M of order D by arXiv:2603.18168 Theorem 1.1). The authors believe only D/(ML) → 0 is necessary and leave the other shape constraints open (p 5). At initialization see also Li–Nica–Roy (T262).",
 src='L.-P. Chaintron, L. Chizat and J. Maass, ResNets of All Shapes and Sizes: Convergence of Training Dynamics in the Large-scale Limit, arXiv:2603.18168 (2026); B. Bordelon, L. Noci, M. B. Li, B. Hanin and C. Pehlevan, Depthwise Hyperparameter Transfer in Residual Networks: Dynamics and Scaling Limit, arXiv:2309.16620 (2023); G. Yang, D. Yu, C. Zhu and S. Hayou, Tensor Programs VI: Feature Learning in Infinite-Depth Neural Networks, arXiv:2310.02244 (2023)',
 loc='2603.18168 Theorem 1.1 (p 4) and the shape-constraint remark (p 5); 2310.02244 Theorem 4.1; 2309.16620 Appendix F (p 30).',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.16620',
 why='Depth-muP is how large residual models are scaled; a rigorous joint limit would justify transfer of hyperparameters across depth.',
 st='Partly solved: joint limit for depth-two blocks with D = O(M) on fixed horizons (2603.18168, 2026); the shape, horizon and depth-one-block cases are open.',
 n='Fame: active 2023-26 topic at the boundary of SDE limits and training theory.',
 a=True),
]
